{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## BROADENED (operator): map TYPES, WORKFLOWS and STATUSES too — validate every vocabulary at once\n\nThe generalisation: **rebar hardcodes four vocabularies of instance strings, and each one is an\nindependent mismatch class.** From `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py`:\n\n    LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA   Highest / High / Medium / Low / Lowest\n    LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA       the issue-type names\n    LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA     To Do / In Progress / Done\n    RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK    Blocks / Relates  (+ an inward/outward direction flag)\n\nEvery entry is a literal that must EXIST on the instance, with that exact spelling. Today the only\nway we learn one is wrong is a live cell going red. One boot can check all four.\n\n### This class has ALREADY cost this epic twice, which is the argument for including it\n\n * **[rebar:7f93]** — outbound status silently failed because **a transition's NAME is not its\n   destination STATUS name.** `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` maps `in_progress` → `\"In Progress\"` (a state\n   name), while DC's classic workflow offers transitions called `Start Progress` and `Done`. Matching\n   on transition name alone missed, got soft-failed into `bridge_alerts`, and the pass exited 0 with\n   the status unchanged. It never fired on Cloud because the DIG workflow names transitions after\n   their destinations, so the two spellings coincide.\n * **[rebar:d067]** — outbound status submitted as a field edit rather than a transition at all.\n\nSo the map must capture BOTH spellings, per issue type, or it will reproduce exactly the blind spot\nthat produced 7f93.\n\n### Added to the checklist\n\n**Issue types (per project, as created):**\n * every issue type the default project yields, with `subtask: true/false` — the sub-task flag is\n   what `set_parent` branches on\n * whether each name in `LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA` exists\n\n**Workflows and statuses (per issue type — they can differ):**\n * which workflow is bound to each issue type\n * every status in that workflow, with its **name AND its status-category** (`To Do` / `In Progress`\n   / `Done` categories are distinct from status NAMES, and inbound classification may rely on either)\n * for each status, the **transitions available FROM it**, recording for each the **transition name\n   AND its destination status name** — the 7f93 pair, side by side\n * whether every value in `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` is reachable, and by which transition from which\n   starting state. A status that exists but is unreachable from the initial state is a different\n   defect from a misspelled one, and both are silent today\n\n**Priorities and link types:**\n * `/rest/api/2/priority` — does every `LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA` value exist?\n * `/rest/api/2/issueLinkType` — does every `RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK` name exist, **and is the\n   inward/outward direction flag correct?** A right name with an inverted direction produces a link\n   that points the wrong way and would pass any name-only check\n\n### The output shape that makes this useful\n\nNot prose. A structured artifact whose most valuable section is a **direct diff: every rebar\nvocabulary entry against what the instance offers**, with three verdicts per entry — present /\nabsent / present-but-different (wrong spelling, wrong direction, unreachable). That diff is\nimmediately actionable and is what the harness's startup assertion should later enforce.\n\n**Report, do not fix.** If the map finds `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` disagrees with the instance, that is\na finding for a ticket — possibly a product bug, possibly a harness-config gap — and deciding which\nis not the mapping job's call."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785798388272510001,"uuid":"c3f81159-bd54-4332-921a-af6db53729a1"}